# Security headers for the public web client. Included, not inherited - see the note below.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS IS A TEMPLATE. `__CONNECT_SRC__` is substituted by 40-render-csp.sh
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The rendered file lands at /etc/nginx/security-headers.conf. The one placeholder is the whole of
# `connect-src`, because the API origin is per-deployment: a self-hoster points the client at their
# own server and the policy has to follow. See 40-render-csp.sh for the validation and the
# https -> wss derivation, and docker/web/README.md for the deployer-facing story.
#
# Editing this file by hand inside a running container does nothing: the entrypoint rewrites the
# rendered copy on every start.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why this is a separate file rather than a block at server level
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# nginx's `add_header` does not merge across levels. A location that declares even one
# `add_header` of its own stops inheriting *every* `add_header` from the enclosing server block.
# Since almost every location in nginx.conf sets its own Cache-Control, a server-level CSP would
# have been silently dropped on exactly the responses that carry the app - and a CSP that is
# absent looks identical to a CSP that is satisfied. So the whole set lives here and is
# `include`d at each level that needs it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Content-Security-Policy, directive by directive
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# This is a hard requirement, not hardening. On the web build the account master key and the MLS
# signing keys live in IndexedDB rather than an OS keychain (SecureStore.hardwareBacked is false
# on web, and the key-backup UI says so), which means any script that runs on this origin can
# read them. CSP is what stands between "an injected string" and "an attacker holds the user's
# private keys". See the Security posture section of
# docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-11-browser-only-build-design.md.
#
#   default-src 'none'
#       Deny by default and enumerate. Every fetch destination below is listed explicitly, so a
#       new one fails visibly in the console instead of being quietly permitted by a broad
#       fallback. It is the more brittle choice and the correct one for this payload.
#
#   script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval' https://js.stripe.com
#       The load-bearing line. No 'unsafe-inline', no 'unsafe-eval', and one external script host:
#       the app's own bundles plus Stripe.js. Angular emits no inline script, and the one inline
#       event handler the production build *does* emit - Beasties' `onload="this.media='all'"` on
#       the deferred stylesheet link - is rewritten away at image-build time precisely so that
#       'unsafe-inline' is not needed here. See the rewrite and its assertion in
#       .github/workflows/web-client-image.yml.
#
#       https://js.stripe.com is the one host allowed to serve script here, and it is not
#       negotiable in either direction: Stripe does not support self-hosting v3 and 3DS breaks when
#       it is served from another origin, so the checkout either loads from Stripe's origin or does
#       not load. Its failure mode is a blank card form with a console error nobody reads.
#
#       The cost is real and accepted rather than argued away: script from js.stripe.com runs on
#       this origin and can reach the same IndexedDB the rest of this policy exists to protect.
#       There is no arrangement that takes card payments in our own UI without it, and the
#       alternative - a PAN input on our own origin - is worse on this axis as well as on PCI
#       scope. Nothing else may be added to this directive on the same reasoning.
#
#       'wasm-unsafe-eval' is required and is not a weakening of the above. Compiling a
#       WebAssembly module counts as dynamic code generation under CSP, so without it
#       `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` on venta_crypto_bg.wasm is refused and the client has
#       no crypto engine at all. The token exists specifically so that wasm can be allowed
#       *without* allowing `eval` and `new Function` on JavaScript, which 'unsafe-eval' would.
#
#   style-src 'self' 'nonce-$request_id'
#       A per-response nonce, generated by nginx and substituted into the HTML by the sub_filter
#       in nginx.conf. A hash set was considered and rejected: the built index.html carries two
#       <style> blocks - the loading-spinner block from src/index.html and the critical CSS
#       Beasties inlines - and the second one's bytes change with every build, so a pinned hash
#       would go stale on any style change and take the app's first paint with it. On top of that
#       Angular injects component styles as <style> elements at runtime, which no build-time hash
#       can cover. The nonce covers all three at once and is Angular's own documented mechanism
#       for it (`ngCspNonce`).
#
#   style-src-attr 'unsafe-inline'
#       Stated separately rather than folded into style-src, because the difference matters. The
#       templates use literal `style="..."` attributes in ~200 places; CSP nonces do not apply to
#       attributes, so nothing short of 'unsafe-inline' permits them and the alternative is a
#       visibly broken layout. Confining it to `-attr` means an injected string still cannot
#       introduce a <style> element or pull in a stylesheet, and style has no path to reading
#       IndexedDB regardless - script does, and script is strict.
#
#   connect-src   (rendered from $VENTA_API_HOSTS - see 40-render-csp.sh)
#       'self' and blob: (several upload paths re-fetch an object URL), then two entries per
#       configured API host - `https://<host>` for the REST API and `wss://<host>` for the SignalR
#       hub, which realtime-connection.service.ts builds as `${baseUrl}/api/v1/ws/hub` and then
#       upgrades. The wss form is derived from the https one rather than being asked for
#       separately, because supplying only one is the obvious mistake and it fails as "no realtime
#       events ever arrive", which is indistinguishable from a backend outage.
#
#       Then the fixed, code-derived hosts: Klipy for GIF search (gif.service.ts), the Sentry
#       ingest host from the DSN in main.ts, the three AI providers the assistant features call
#       directly from the browser with `dangerouslyAllowBrowser: true`, and api.stripe.com, which
#       Stripe.js calls from this document to confirm a payment. Our own billing endpoints are not
#       Stripe's - they are on the API host above - so this entry is only ever used by Stripe.js.
#
#       Configurable, but never wildcard. api-config.service.ts lets a user point the client at a
#       self-hosted server (`setServer(domain)` -> `https://<domain>`), so a single hard-coded
#       origin breaks self-hosting outright - and the tempting fix, `connect-src https:`, would
#       hand any injected script a free exfiltration channel for the master key and the MLS
#       signing keys sitting in IndexedDB. Naming the permitted origins is the middle path: the
#       default is exactly api.venta.gg and its socket, a deployer widens it deliberately, and a
#       malformed value stops the container instead of silently producing a looser policy.
#
#       ICE and TURN are absent on purpose, not by omission: CSP has no directive that governs
#       WebRTC ICE candidates, so the STUN/TURN servers in environment.ts need no entry and voice
#       is unaffected by this policy.
#
#   img-src 'self' data: blob: https:
#       Broad by necessity. Messages embed images and GIFs from arbitrary third-party hosts, and
#       an allowlist of image CDNs is not knowable from the client. data: is the QR codes,
#       blob: the local previews and authenticated image fetches.
#
#   frame-src
#       The five players embed-player.component.ts will frame, and only those. That component
#       already maintains the allowlist; this mirrors it so that a bypass of the TypeScript check
#       still cannot frame an arbitrary origin.
#
#       Plus the two Stripe origins. js.stripe.com is the Payment Element itself - the iframe the
#       card number is typed into, which is the entire reason the PAN never enters our DOM.
#       hooks.stripe.com is the 3D Secure challenge, framed by Stripe.js when the issuer asks for
#       one; omitting it passes the ordinary test cards and fails every real European payment.
#
#   frame-ancestors 'none'
#       This app must never be framed: a page holding key material inside someone else's frame is
#       a clickjacking target. X-Frame-Options below says the same thing for anything that
#       predates CSP2.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What is deliberately NOT here
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy and Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy. They are what you add to unlock
# SharedArrayBuffer, and nothing here needs it: venta-crypto compiles to single-threaded wasm
# with no rayon and no threads feature, so cross-origin isolation buys this deployment nothing.
# It would cost something real - COEP: require-corp blocks every cross-origin subresource that
# does not opt in with CORP or CORS headers, which is exactly the third-party image and GIF
# embeds img-src is broad for above. Adding them would break message embeds to buy nothing.
#
add_header Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'none'; script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval' https://js.stripe.com; style-src 'self' 'nonce-$request_id'; style-src-attr 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: blob: https:; font-src 'self' data:; connect-src __CONNECT_SRC__; media-src 'self' blob: data: https:; frame-src https://www.youtube-nocookie.com https://www.youtube.com https://player.vimeo.com https://player.twitch.tv https://open.spotify.com https://js.stripe.com https://hooks.stripe.com; worker-src 'self' blob:; manifest-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'" always;

# Content sniffing turns a user-uploaded file served as one type into script of another. Off.
add_header X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff" always;

# frame-ancestors already covers this for anything current; kept for older engines.
add_header X-Frame-Options "DENY" always;

# Paths in this app carry guild, channel and message ids. Sending them to third-party hosts in a
# Referer - including every embedded image and GIF host img-src allows - leaks the shape of a
# user's private conversations. `strict-origin-when-cross-origin` would still send the origin;
# no-referrer sends nothing, and nothing here needs a referrer to work.
add_header Referrer-Policy "no-referrer" always;

# Voice and screen share need these, so they are granted to this origin explicitly rather than
# left to the browser default. Named because getting it wrong fails as "the microphone button
# does nothing", with no console error that points at a policy. `self` and not `*`: an embedded
# third-party player must not inherit microphone or display capture.
#
# `payment` is delegated, and to exactly one third party. It used to be `payment=()`, which
# disables the Payment Request API for this origin entirely - card entry still worked, and Apple
# Pay, Google Pay and Link simply never rendered inside the Payment Element, with no error
# anywhere. That is the worst kind of failure: a checkout that looks finished and quietly converts
# worse than it should. `payment=(self "https://js.stripe.com")` grants the capability to this
# document and to the Stripe iframe that actually needs it, and to nothing else - the wallet sheet
# is opened from inside js.stripe.com, so naming `self` alone is not enough.
#
# This is the one capability on this line that is handed to an origin we do not control, and it is
# the same origin script-src already trusts for the same reason. Nothing else may be added here on
# the strength of that precedent.
add_header Permissions-Policy "microphone=(self), camera=(self), display-capture=(self), geolocation=(), payment=(self \"https://js.stripe.com\")" always;
